#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:5
msgid "Spyder Unittest"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:7
msgid "**Spyder-unittest** is a plugin that allows you to run automatic tests within Spyder and view the results."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:9
msgid "Spyder Unittest plugin in Spyder"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:12
msgid "Spyder-unittest can run tests written using the ``unittest`` module in the Python library or the ``pytest`` library."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:20
msgid "Installing the plugin"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:22
msgid "If you installed Spyder using conda, the best way to install Spyder-unittest is to run the following command in your Terminal or Anaconda prompt on Windows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:35
msgid "When the Unittest plugin is installed, it will be available under the menu item :menuselection:`View --> Panes --> Unittest`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:37
msgid "Spyder showing View Panes Unittest"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:40
msgid "You will see it then as a tab next to the :guilabel:`Files` tab."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:42
msgid "If you plan to use the ``pytest`` library, then you also need to make sure that the ``pytest`` library is installed. This needs to be done in the Python environment that Spyder uses to run your code, which can be specified under :menuselection:`Preferences --> Python interpreter`. The ``pyzeromq`` library also needs to be installed in the same environment. However, this is likely already the case, because you need the ``spyder-kernels`` to be installed in order for Spyder to use the environment and ``spyder-kernels`` depends on ``pyzeromq``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:53
msgid "Running tests"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:55
msgid "The first step is to write the automatic tests. Follow the usual instructions for the framework that you are using:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:58
msgid "If you use the ``unittest`` module in the standard Python library, see this `basic example <basic example_>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:59
msgid "If you use the ``pytest`` library, see this `quick example <quick example_>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:65
msgid "After the automatic tests are written, they can be executed. To run the tests, either use :menuselection:`Run --> Run unit tests` menu item or click on the \"Start\" button with the green triangle (▷) icon in the Unit testing pane. The first time that you run the tests, a configuration window will pop up where you need to specify the testing framework. All options in the configuration window are explained in the section :ref:`unittest-configuration` below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:70
msgid "Spyder running tests using the Run button and the Run menu item"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:73
msgid "After running the tests, the results will appear in the Unit Testing pane. If you use the ``unittest`` module, then the results will only appear after all the tests have been run. If you use the ``pytest`` library, then the results will be continuously updated, as shown in the image below. A summary of the results is displayed at the top of the Unit Testing pane; in the image below, this is \"1 test failed, 3 passed, 1 other\". If no tests are found, then the summary will read \"No results to show\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:79
msgid "Unit Testing pane with test results coming in slowly"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:88
msgid "Viewing test results"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:90
msgid "The Unit Testing pane shows the results after running the tests. The top shows a summary of test results in the form \"xxx tests passed, xxx failed, xxx other\". The results of the individual tests are displayed in a table occupying most of the pane."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:94
msgid "Unit testing pane with test results"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:97
msgid "Every row in the table corresponds to one test. The color indicates the result of that test: green for success, red for failure, and yellow for the rest. The table has four columns:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:101
msgid "The first column shows the outcome of the test. This is commonly \"passed\" or \"failed\", but there are more possibilities depending on the testing framework."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:103
msgid "The second column shows the name of the test. To save space, components of the test name are abbreviated in a way that still makes the names unique. If you hoover over the abbreviated test name, then a tool tip with the full test name will appear."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:106
msgid "The third column may show some extra information, for instance an error message if the test failed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:107
msgid "The fourth column shows the time (in milliseconds) that it took to run the test. The ``unittest`` module does not report the time, so if you use the ``unittest`` module as your testing framework then this column will be empty."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:110
msgid "Initially, the test results are shown in the order in which the tests are run. You can sort the test results on one of the four columns by clicking on the column header. In particular, you can sort on the outcome of the test by clicking on the header of the first column, as shown in the picture below. This allows you to quickly locate failing tests if you have a big test suite with many tests."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:115
msgid "Unit Testing pane with many test results; after sorting by outcome, the one test failure appears at the top"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:118
msgid "Some tests, especially failing tests, have more than one line of information. To display all the output, This extra information is not shown by default. To expand a row and show the extra information of a particular test, click on the icon at the start of the line. The options menu of the Unit testing pane (\"Hamburger\" icon at top right) also contains the item :menuselection:`Expand all`, which expands all the rows in the table. The menu item :menuselection:`Collapse all` does the opposite."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:125
msgid "Expanding and collapsing test results in the Unit Testing pane"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:128
msgid "If you double click on a test result, then the :ref:`panes-editor` pane will jump to the location where this test is defined. Unfortunately, this functionality is only available if you use the ``pytest`` framework. The ``unittest`` module does not record the test location."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:132
msgid "Spyder's editor displaying the source code of a test after doubling clicking on it in the Unit testing pane"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:135
msgid "Finally, if you want to look at the raw output of the test run, then click on the :menuselection:`Show output` item in the options menu of the Unit testing pane. This is a good troubleshooting tool if the results are not what you expect, for instance if no tests are found while you are certain that you wrote some tests."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:138
msgid "Spyder shows the output of the test run after clicking on the \"Show output\" menu item"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:148
msgid "Configuration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:150
msgid "You can change the configuration of the spyder-unittest plugin by clicking on the :menuselection:`Configure` item in the options menu of the Unit testing pane. This will show the configuration window, shown below. The configuration window will also appear whenever there is no valid configuration for the plugin, for instance on the first time that you run tests."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:154
msgid "The test configuration window"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:157
msgid "There are two important configuration options that you need to set correctly. Firstly, you need to pick the testing framework. You can use either the ``unittest`` module in the standard Python library or the ``pytest`` library. Actually, there is a third possibility for the testing framework, the ``nose`` library, but this choice is deprecated and we plan to remove it in the next version of the plugin."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:162
msgid "Secondly, you need to specify the directory in which the tests are stored. Spyder will find all the test in the specified directory and its subdirectories (at any level) according to the test discovery rules of the testing framework that you are using."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:165
msgid "The plugin can also display testing coverage: which lines of your code are executed when the tests are run. This functionality is only available when using the ``pytest`` framework. You also need to install the ``pytest-cov`` library in the Python environment that Spyder uses to run your code before you can do coverage testing. There is an option in the configuration window to turn on coverage testing. After running the tests, the coverage results will be displayed under all the test results."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:171
msgid "Unit testing pane showing test results and coverage"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:174
msgid "Spyder saves the testing configuration sp that you do not have to specify it every time that you want to run the tests. If you use Spyder :ref:`panes-projects`, then the testing configuration is associated to the currently open project and will be stored with the project configuration. If no project is open, then the testing configuration is stored in the global Spyder configuration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:178
msgid "The :menuselection:`Dependencies` item in the options menu of the Unit testing pane displays a window showing which testing frameworks are installed in the Python environment that Spyder uses to run tests. The window also shows any plugins that come with the testing framework and the versions of the testing frameworks and the plugins. A side-effect of this command is that Spyder will pick up any testing frameworks that were installed since Spyder was started."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins/unittest.rst:182
msgid "The Unittest dependencies window"
msgstr ""

